{"id":"u-13448ec1-01e4-4791-a95f-3c14dedee138","src":"community","title":"Level Up (5Day Female)","author":"Chris","url":"https://www.boostcamp.app/users/XExx06-5day-female-aesthetic-program","desc":"Hi besties! This is a common workout split among gym girlies. It's a highly glute focused program, if that's what you're looking for. It's often locked behind apps and subscriptions and nonsense, but no gatekeeping here. Its basic layout and structure is thanks to Bret Contreras (BootyByBret), and long term commitment to it built a lot of influencer's bodies. \n\nBasic Split:\n\nDay 1 - Glutes & Hamstrings\nDay 2 - Back & Biceps\nDay 3 - Quads, Core & Calves\nDay 4 - Delts & Triceps\nDay 5 - Glutes & Hamstrings\n\nBasic Volume:\nVolume is measured in hard sets per week and represents the total “growth signal” sent to your body. Muscle growth is primarily driven by training close to failure, which creates high mechanical tension in the muscle. That tension signals your body to build new muscle tissue. Volume (sets/wk), is how much of this signal you are sending.\n\nGlutes - Very High (~30ish sets/wk)\nDelts, Quads, Hams (~10ish sets/wk)\nEverything Else (~5-10ish sets/wk)\n\nIntensity:\n\nRPE is a scale that counts how close a rep is to failing. Failure where you attempt to do a rep and your muscles are unable to produce the force to do so. \n\nRPE 10 = could not do more reps or load\nRPE 9 = could do 1 more rep\nRPE 8 = could do 2 more reps\nRPE 7 = could do 3 more reps\nRPE 6 = could do 4 more reps etc.\n\nIn this program, each exercise includes a final AMRAP set (“as many reps as possible”) to gauge your proximity to failure. Earlier sets build volume; the last set ensures accurate RPE.\n\nEg.\nSet 1 – 8–12 reps @ RPE 7  \nSet 2 – 8–12 reps @ RPE 7  \nSet 3 – 8–12 reps @ RPE 9 (AMRAP test)\n\nThe program is also designed around evidence-based hypertrophy principles, combining block periodization with practical auto-regulation (RPE). It emphasizes high-quality training close to failure, strategic volume management, and progressive adaptation across distinct training phases.\n\nPeriodization: Training Blocks\n\nThe program is organised into block-periodized mesocycles, each lasting several weeks. This is mostly for fun and to give structure and guidance for starting.\n\n1. Accumulation Block\nFocus: Adaptation to volume and learning RPE accuracy.\nThe first two weeks serve as an “intro phase” to help your body adjust.\nThe next two weeks push toward higher-quality sets and precise RPE control.\n\n2. Intensification Block\nFocus: Increasing load and refining execution.\nSlightly lower reps and RPEs to manage fatigue while maintaining tension.\nRepresents the “steady-state” of long-term training—balanced and sustainable. You can just copy the week and run it indefinitely if you'd like.\n\n3. Testing Week\nA short phase to evaluate progress and reset motivation.\nOption to swap exercises, assess RPE accuracy, and plan the next cycle.","level":["Beginner","Intermediate"],"goals":["Muscle","Women's"],"equipment":["Full Gym"],"minutes":60,"joins":4,"rate":0,"rateCount":0,"updated":"2026-03-15T02:08:42.478Z","variations":[{"name":"default","days":["Glutes & Hamstrings","Back & Biceps","Quads & Core","Shoulders & Triceps","Glutes & Hamstrings"],"weeks":[[{"ex":[{"n":"Hip Thrust (Smith Machine)","sets":[{"lo":6,"hi":10,"rpe":6},{"lo":6,"hi":10,"rpe":8}],"note":"Hip Thrust Variant (Glute Max)"},{"n":"Romanian Deadlift (Barbell)","sets":[{"lo":6,"hi":10,"rpe":6},{"lo":6,"hi":10,"rpe":8}],"note":"Hip Hinge Variant (Glute Max, Hamstrings & Spinal Erectors)"},{"n":"Step-Ups (Cable)","sets":[{"lo":8,"hi":12,"rpe":6},{"lo":8,"hi":12,"rpe":8}],"note":"Single Leg Variant (Glute Max, Quads, Glute Med/Min)"},{"n":"Abduction (Seated)","sets":[{"lo":12,"hi":16,"rpe":7},{"lo":12,"hi":16,"rpe":9}],"note":"Abduction Variant (Glute Med/Min)"}]},{"ex":[{"n":"Seated Row (Cable)","sets":[{"lo":8,"hi":12,"rpe":7},{"lo":8,"hi":12,"rpe":9}],"note":"Hor Pull (Upper Back)"},{"n":"Lat Pulldown (Single Arm)","sets":[{"lo":10,"hi":15,"rpe":7},{"lo":10,"hi":15,"rpe":9}],"note":"Ver Pull (Lats)"},{"n":"Reverse Pec Deck","sets":[{"lo":10,"hi":15,"rpe":7},{"lo":10,"hi":15,"rpe":9}],"note":"Rear Delts"},{"n":"Cable Curl (Facing Away)","sets":[{"lo":10,"hi":15,"rpe":7},{"lo":10,"hi":15,"rpe":9}],"note":"Biceps"},{"n":"Behind The Back Lateral Raise (Cable)","sets":[{"lo":10,"hi":15,"rpe":7},{"lo":10,"hi":15,"rpe":9}],"note":"Med. Delts"},{"n":"Stair Climber","sets":[{"sec":600,"secHi":1200,"rpe":5}],"note":"Cardio / Glute Work Capacity. If you're too sore, walk on a high incline or use the elliptical. 7/10 difficulty. Try keep track and slowly increase speed over time."}]},{"ex":[{"n":"Leg Press (45 Degrees)","sets":[{"lo":6,"hi":10,"rpe":5},{"lo":6,"hi":10,"rpe":7}],"note":"Squat Variant (Quads, Adductors, Glute Max)"},{"n":"Reverse Lunge (Smith Machine)","sets":[{"lo":8,"hi":12,"rpe":6},{"lo":8,"hi":12,"rpe":8}],"note":"Single Leg Variant (Glute Max, Quads, Glute Med/Min)"},{"n":"Leg Extension","sets":[{"lo":10,"hi":15,"rpe":7},{"lo":10,"hi":15,"rpe":9}],"note":"Leg Ext (Quads--rectus\nfemoris)"},{"n":"Leg Press Calf Raise","sets":[{"lo":10,"hi":15,"rpe":7},{"lo":10,"hi":15,"rpe":9}]},{"n":"Cable Crunch","sets":[{"lo":10,"hi":15,"rpe":7},{"lo":10,"hi":15,"rpe":9}]}]},{"ex":[{"n":"Seated Overhead Press (Dumbbell)","sets":[{"lo":8,"hi":12,"rpe":6},{"lo":8,"hi":12,"rpe":8}],"note":"Ver Push (Front Delts)"},{"n":"Lying Cable Lateral Raise","sets":[{"lo":8,"hi":12,"rpe":6},{"lo":8,"hi":12,"rpe":8}],"note":"Med. Delts"},{"n":"Dip (Bodyweight)","sets":[{"amrap":1,"rpe":6},{"amrap":1,"rpe":8}],"note":"Triceps (Dec Push to hit chest, front delts and triceps)"},{"n":"Face Pull","sets":[{"lo":10,"hi":15,"rpe":6},{"lo":10,"hi":15,"rpe":8}],"note":"Rear Delts"},{"n":"Stair Climber","sets":[{"sec":600,"secHi":1200,"rpe":5}],"note":"Cardio / Glute Work Capacity. If you're too sore, walk on a high incline or use the elliptical. 7/10 difficulty. Try